{"reference":"Job 3","book":"Job","chapter":3,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Protest without easy answers","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Job’s speech gives full voice to grief, anger, confusion, and a longing for death. It does not turn suffering into evidence of secret wrongdoing or require a person in anguish to be silent. The chapter therefore resists quick explanations and leaves room for protest within speech directed toward God.","verse_refs":["Job 3:1-26"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_003_c7a2f7462c995dc12833c094"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"What begins here","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"This lament follows seven days of shared silence and opens the extended debate between Job and Eliphaz, Bildad, and Zophar. As the debate unfolds, the friends increasingly explain Job’s suffering through wrongdoing, while Job rejects that diagnosis. His cry here is therefore the beginning of a sustained argument, not a settled explanation of his suffering.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_003_313542747436dbe88e96abc9"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_3","text":"Later readers have connected Job with endurance and the Lord’s compassionate outcome. That description should not flatten this book into a demand for silent suffering: Job’s persistence includes protest, and it must not be used to prevent escape, protection, or help in situations of abuse.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_003_44e7c3a9c4c95da47773fd0e"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}